mechaber.en:
Her husband went and she borrowed and ate — he is obligated to pay.

{Rama: The lender sues the woman and she sues her husband; and if the woman is not here — sue the husband (Ran ch. 5 of Dayanei Gezeirot). However, if the woman waived to her husband — the lender has nothing (Mordechai ch. Yesh Nochalin). If beit din assessed sustenance for her and ordered someone to sustain her and collect from the husband's property — if the husband died he has no claim against her. But if they said plainly to lend to her — if the husband died or has nothing to pay and she inherited from elsewhere — she must pay (so implied from responsum of Rashba brought by Beit Yosef). If someone on his own sustained her from his own — the husband is not obligated to pay him, and behold he lost his money.

{Rama: And there is no distinction between her father and another who sustained her; however, if they were obligated to the husband and sustained his wife — they deduct from his debt (all this in Hagahot Mordechai on Kesubos). And if her father stood and sustained his daughter along with the groom — nevertheless he need not pay him except for his own sustenance and not his daughter's (Terumat HaDeshen siman 317). If she did not borrow but sold her property and sustained herself — she has no claim against him, and her handiwork is hers (Ran beginning of Dayanei Gezeirot).}

If she stood in judgment and they assessed sustenance for her and beit din sold and gave her, or she sold for herself, and the husband came and said "I left her sustenance" — behold she swears while holding a sacred object that he did not leave her sustenance. And if she did not claim and did not sell but waited until he came and said "I left you sustenance," and she says "you did not leave but I borrowed from so-and-so and was sustained" — he swears a heses oath that he left her sustenance and is released, and the debt remains upon her.

{Rama: (But a man is not believed to say he gave her for the future) (Ran beginning of Almanah Nezunah). And if she sold movable property and said she sold for sustenance, and he claims and says "I left your sustenance" — she swears a heses oath that he did not leave.

{Rama: If he claimed and said she should have used her handiwork for her sustenance — if she borrowed without beit din and she is a craftswoman whose handiwork suffices for large matters even if not for small matters — his claim is a valid claim. But if beit din assessed for her — her handiwork must suffice for everything (all this in Tur).}
